#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176669} :activity/id #uuid "503ab0f0-15ff-5bd2-ac55-6138d76918dd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050698 :topic/id #uuid "5fd0d001-91cd-5166-a989-8a388a0618c0" :topic/math-academy-id 3569} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176669 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176973 :tutorial/content "In a rectangular (or Cartesian) system of coordinates, we can think of a point as the vertex of a rectangular solid. For instance, the point $P(1,\\sqrt3,3)$ can be represented as follows:\n\n![](images/47/47873274f1cc630a8275b3ef2f83210b3d16a4f3d0acabeae9dbda32844a9adf.png)\n\nWhen we consider the corresponding cylindrical coordinates of $P,$ given by $\\left(2, \\dfrac{\\pi}3, 3\\right)$ in this case, we can think of $P$ as a point that lies on the edge of a cylinder, as shown below.\n\n![](images/9f/9fefa7c5fac5cac803fdebeaa9eab4233d723b452d2ee8dc17151d992064223f.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "7224d97c-31fc-5a6c-986c-9f8fd6dae5de" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3941 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7e3fef5c-90c1-5ae0-8dd6-f78ba4880000" :step/math-academy-id 23340 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176713}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176698 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175964 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175814 :question/id #uuid "52199b9c-bb04-5c14-b4ca-909d46c7f039" :question/math-academy-id "e-8808"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a3e455da-2c31-55af-8122-6fa0e5020404" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364674} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399426}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Surface by Its Polar Equation"} :step/id #uuid "fa14dab6-6b2c-5517-80c3-face079514f6" :step/math-academy-id 32906 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176714}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176699 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175965 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175815 :question/id #uuid "ee821db7-b420-5f37-a54c-a0bbe6cc83be" :question/math-academy-id "e-8809"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eae7e5be-e883-5658-84a0-5de5da567887" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049694}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362472}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Polar Equation of a Given Surface"} :step/id #uuid "ce566092-6969-5011-a590-ad519430622e" :step/math-academy-id 32907 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176698}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176712 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175976 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175826 :question/id #uuid "f948d39d-6d7d-56b8-bb2c-b0a74ac514b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-8893"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4472e5-68af-5e8c-83cb-ceab2a6dfe1e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240833} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240835} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399433}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cones and Half-Cones"} :step/id #uuid "605436cb-a92c-5db6-8a0c-283fbd5bc112" :step/math-academy-id 33203 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176715}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176713 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176985 :tutorial/content "Recall that for a point $(r,\\theta,z)$ in cylindrical polar coordinates,\n\n- $r\\geq 0$ and $\\theta\\in [0,2\\pi)$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWhen we fix one of these coordinates and allow the others to vary, we obtain some simple surfaces.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\nr=c, \\qquad\n$$\n\n where $c\\geq 0$ requires that the distance from any point on the surface to the $z$-axis is fixed while $\\theta$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. So, we obtain a circular cylinder.\n\n![](images/72/728776f0f04642a1596df605968cb5dc5df22ea0a401cf9522ba1ff91839ff0e.png)\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n\\theta=c\n$$\n\n where $c\\in [0,2\\pi)$ requires that if we project the vector $\\overrightarrow{OP}$ onto the $xy$-plane for every point $P$ on our surface, the resulting vector makes an angle of $c$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. This constructs a **half-plane** that passes through the $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/bd/bdc54b001fde4816ced9eff3a771bdd5900804f78b3bd5e9586db42bebe80297.png)\n\n- Finally, the equation \n\n$$\nz=c\n$$\n\n where $z\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ requires that the $z$-coordinate of any point on our surface is fixed while $\\theta$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. Therefore, this surface is a plane parallel to the $xy$-plane.\n\n![](images/c3/c3f0af5747690797f27e156aa281c0b24f600919961a63a0244febaca710fcbe.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "16974c3a-8773-51dd-9c56-6407a423727f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5308 :tutorial/title "Describing Simple Surfaces in Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "3218f617-0ff9-5905-a133-72f0abbba8d8" :step/math-academy-id 33206 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176699}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176714 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176986 :tutorial/content "What surface is defined by the equation $z^2=r^2$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?\n\nAgain, recall that for a point $(r,\\theta,z)$ in cylindrical polar coordinates,\n\n- $r\\geq 0$ and $\\theta\\in [0,2\\pi)$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nz^2=r^2\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the square of the distance from any point on the surface to the $xy$-plane equals the square of the distance from that point to the $z$-axis.\nIn other words, the tangent of the angle between any position vector on the surface and the $z$-axis, which is given by $\\dfrac{r}{z}$, must be equal to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{r}{z} =\\pm \\sqrt{\\dfrac{r^2}{z^2}} = \\pm\\sqrt{\\dfrac{r^2}{r^2}} = \\pm1.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, $\\theta$ can vary arbitrarily in its domain. \n\n- For $\\dfrac{r}{z} = 1,$ the angle between the position vector of any point on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$ This corresponds to a *half-cone* for positive $z{:}$\n\n![](images/18/1841d50bb07c0e6787f4824af0e13516912fb9ad77fac0515f00c243899eb18e.png)\n\n- On the other hand, for $\\dfrac{r}{z} = -1,$ the angle between the position vector of any point on the surface and the $z$-axis must be equal $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}.$ This corresponds to a *half-cone* for negative $z{:}$\n\n![](images/e3/e32b1b4ba84faea7a4dc536e0f49bcda9295a4a42790162b3b9bad06db39a908.png)\n\nTherefore, combining the two half cones, the equation $z^2 = r^2$ defines the *cone* shown below.\n\n![](images/84/842f8cf967d10144e568804bdfb1aadf75f029cf87dd7b6785168a44a95d0615.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "795b19b5-f3e0-535a-99a9-cd09e1c2cb58" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5309 :tutorial/title "Cones in Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "3c69de56-41f5-581e-b36e-f6ebf219b307" :step/math-academy-id 33207 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176712}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176715 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176987 :tutorial/content "So far, we have kept the domains of the plane polar coordinates restricted to $r\\geq 0$ and $\\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).$ What happens if we lift these restrictions?\n\nConsider the equation $r=\\theta$ with the domain restrictions $r\\geq 0$ and $\\theta\\in [0,2\\pi)$ which defines the surface shown below.\n\n![](images/84/84737b12a1689fe2ac03ab09572e4c396d35493f55df5d65af80eeb8c5ae5676.png)\n\nSuppose we remove the restriction $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$ so that it can be any number. What would the surface look like then?\n\nIf we remove the restriction on $\\theta,$ we obtain more spirals. For example, the diagram below shows $r=\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in [0, 4\\pi).$\n\n![](images/56/568291daf47340265d97a121f0b7b927233f2698e87e0d5adebbba640ff20afe.png)\n\nWe can also lift the restriction $r \\geq 0$ so $r$ can take negative values. Typically, allowing negative values of $r$ adds $\\pi$ to the parameter $\\theta.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(|r|,\\theta ),\\, & r\\ge 0, \\\\ (|r|,\\theta +\\pi ),\\, & r<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8c45bbba-d4b2-5559-8236-d79eb000c27b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5310 :tutorial/title "Relaxing Variable Constraints"} :step/id #uuid "f7f59e46-b37b-5d77-aa4e-867545d8bca0" :step/math-academy-id 33211}] :activity/title "Surfaces in Cylindrical Polar Coordinates" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176119}]}
